            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new word: what did nīlaḥ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: प्रातः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "प्रातः (prātaḥ) — \"morning\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "प्रातः (prātaḥ) is early morning: the stretch before the sun stands high, and the hour the first greeting of the day belongs to."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have said pra- at the front of a word twice already, in प्रणामः and in प्रसन्नः, where it carries the sense \"forward, out in front\". प्रातः (prātaḥ) is the front end of the day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek prōi, \"early\", is the same small piece put to the same work, and Latin prae is its cousin; English carries it as the pre- in front of a thousand words."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first part of the day, named."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
