        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: सायम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सायम् (sāyam) — \"evening\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सायम् (sāyam) is evening: the end of the working day, when a thing is set down rather than picked up."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put it beside प्रातः and you hold the two ends of the daylight — the greeting at one end and the leave-taking at the other. sāyaṁ prātaḥ, \"evening and morning\", is one of the oldest paired phrases the language has."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It rests on a piece meaning \"to finish, to let go\", so evening is named as the slackening of the day rather than as its darkness."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both ends of the daylight, and the middle between them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
